-\begin{abstract}
-Fixed owners supply nonnegative resource bundles to a production technology.
-We seek nonnegative payments that cover every coalition and never increase
-when an owner hides resources. For any production function that is the minimum
-of $K$ fixed nonnegative linear forms, we construct a deterministic all-profile
-rule with total payment at most $(1+o(1))T\log K/\log\log K$, uniformly in
-the number of owners and resources, where $T$ is grand production. A matching
-lower bound holds on simple directed acyclic flow networks with distinct
-strategic edge owners, making the leading constant one sharp for the worst
-case with at most $K$ structural cuts. The construction combines own-excluded
-support prices, capped contributions, and endpoint marginal payments.
-It extends to all finite monotone concave homogeneous production functions
-with an $O(n/\log n)$ owner-count bound. For rational finite technologies,
-finite sampling and exact certification yield all-profile guarantees after
-potentially exponential preprocessing, with polynomial subsequent evaluation
-in the stated sample parameters. We retain the sharper special-case analysis
-for weighted multi-glove games and prove limitations of several alternative
-rules, including a polynomial obstruction to the original monotone envelope.
-Complete conventional proofs and reproducible certificates accompany the
-results. Historical novelty and human specialist validation remain open.
-\end{abstract}
-\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} production game; capacity reporting; coalition
-coverage; subsidy; flow game; continuous Banzhaf index; uniform certification.
-
-\section{Introduction}
-Coalitional stability and truthful capacity disclosure place different demands
-on an allocation rule. At one fixed report profile, a production or flow game
-may have an efficient core allocation. Selecting such allocations across all
-profiles can nevertheless reward an owner for hiding resources. We allow an
-external subsidy while preserving every original coalition inequality, and
-ask how the smallest possible total-payment factor grows with technology
-complexity.
-
-Our main model fixes the owners, resource types, and technology. Each owner
-may underreport its own nonnegative resource bundle componentwise; utility is
-its payment, without operating costs. We require a single rule on the whole
-real report domain. In particular, owner identities are never replaced by
-strategic capacity units. Total-payment and subsidy factors are distinct:
-if the former is $B$, the latter is $B-1$.
-
-\paragraph{Main results.}
-Theorem~\ref{prod:upper} gives a factor
-$(1+o(1))\log K/\log\log K$ whenever production is the minimum of $K$ fixed
-nonnegative linear forms. This includes general nonnegative production LPs,
-with arbitrary overlapping owner bundles, and single-commodity flow networks.
-Theorem~\ref{dag:sharp} proves sharpness, including on simple DAGs whose
-auxiliary edges are all strategically owned. The network statement uses
-worst cases with \emph{at most} $K$ structural cuts; no padding argument for
-every exact cut count is assumed. The parameter $K$ can be exponential in a
-compact input, so sharpness in $K$ does not settle the owner-count or
-resource-dimension questions.
-
-The upper uses a supporting form selected with one owner's whole report
-omitted, combined with that owner's endpoint marginal contribution. Each
-form contribution is capped at grand production before payment. Averaging
-these two terms over a short interval of deletion probabilities controls the
-extra marginal budget and obtains leading constant one. The simple-DAG lower
-uses shared fan modules and weighted unilateral report transitions. It
-accounts explicitly for every auxiliary owner's payment; ordinary edge
-subdivision would invalidate the reduction.
-
-The later sections extend the upper to all finite-valued monotone concave
-homogeneous production functions, including discontinuities at boundary
-faces. They give an $O(n/\log n)$ owner bound and preserve an elementary
-resource-dimension bound. The corresponding general lower bounds are only
-$\Omega(\log n/\log\log n)$ and $\Omega(\log d/\log\log d)$, respectively.
-Section~\ref{alg:section} gives exact finite sampling, one-seed guarantees
-simultaneously over all real profiles, and sound and complete certification.
-The sample count and post-certification rational-input evaluation are
-polynomial in the displayed parameters; certification preprocessing can be
-exponential.
-
-\paragraph{Special cases and method boundaries.}
-For weighted multi-glove games, the earlier unrestricted hierarchy and capped
-continuous-Banzhaf mechanism already give the same sharp first-order factor
-in the pool count, with a strictly improved finite-pool probability bound.
-We retain their complete proofs and the sharp proportional-class comparison.
-Nonlinear rules on restricted report domains explain why a proportional
-witness alone cannot prove the unrestricted theorem. The appendices collect
-further finite-pool bounds, structural network guarantees and exact rule
-failures. In particular, the own-report monotone envelope of the original
-continuous-noise network rule has a polynomial-in-$K$ lower obstruction;
-the successful general mechanism is not a pointwise-majorant repair of it.
-Optional protection of a fraction of an individual's best \emph{original}
-core payoff costs at most one additional factor under the stated denominators.
-No subsidized core is substituted for that benchmark.
-
-\subsection{Established ingredients and the proposed contribution}
-Dual-price core allocations in linear production games are classical
-\cite{Owen,SametZemel}; max-flow/min-cut gives the network representation.
-Moretti and Norde~\cite[Definition~3.1, Theorem~3.2]{MorettiNorde} define
-weighted multi-glove games and prove total balancedness, including real-weight
-extensions. The raw functional used in the special-case upper is the absolute
-continuous Banzhaf index of Kurz~\cite[Definition~6.11, arXiv v1]{Kurz};
-we prove the identification and do not claim to introduce that index.
-
-Huang et al.~\cite{Huang} study binding capacity underreports with payment
-utility and no use costs in max-flow games. Their efficient core-selection
-impossibility and truthful efficient mechanisms provide the closest strategic
-comparison. Our requirement retains all coalition inequalities while allowing
-subsidy. The proposed contribution is the joint all-profile incentive,
-coverage and sharp budget guarantee, its matching fixed-owner lower bound,
-and the stated extensions and certificates. The independently familiar
-ingredients---own-excluded selection, probabilistic marginals, concentration,
-convex support theory and uniform convergence---are credited separately.
-
-Fixed-game cost of stability~\cite{Bachrach} addresses a different obstruction:
-the games in our concave-production model already have nonempty cores.
-Here subsidy is forced by linking allocations across reports. Population or
-cross-monotonic profit-sharing schemes, including~\cite{Immorlica,Branzei},
-compare participating-player sets; our incentive condition compares reports
-by one unchanged owner. The inspected Br\^anzei--Solymosi--Tijs working
-version uses unit owners. These distinctions specify a comparison, not a
-certificate that no earlier theorem subsumes our result.
-
-The source ledger additionally records capacity-demand cost sharing,
-resource-monotone network revenue allocations, revised-worth production
-games and full-core projection. Some original-source comparisons remain
-partial. In particular the projection diagnostic is not presented as a
-refutation of an uninspected source's complete model. Historical priority,
-significance, and human specialist assessment remain unresolved.
-
-\paragraph{Reading guide and evidence.}
-The general model and upper come first, followed by the matching simple-DAG
-lower. The subsequent multi-glove sections give the earlier sharp hierarchy
-and finer comparisons. General concave production and the finite implementation
-then complete the main results; technical appendices preserve distinct
-supporting results and exact obstructions. Written proofs establish universal
-claims. Exact finite checks test certificates, identities and bounded instances;
-they are not formal verification of the whole manuscript. AI assistance in
-research, drafting and internal model review is disclosed. No recognized
-major-open-problem resolution or external endorsement is claimed.
